Q: How can I place text boxes on the same line while allowing responsive widths longer than the browser default? A: <p>For anyone reading this question in the future, here is the solution I went with, as well as additional caveats to be aware of.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.flex-container {
  display: flex;
  align-items: end;
}

.input-wrapper {
  min-width: 0;
}

.input-label {
  display: block;
  max-width: fit-content;
}

.input-box {
  max-width: 100%;
  box-sizing: border-box;
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;div class="flex-container"&gt;
  &lt;div class="input-wrapper"&gt;
    &lt;label for="ex1" class="input-label"&gt;First Name&lt;/label&gt;
    &lt;input id="ex1" type="text" class="input-box"/&gt;
  &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
<p>I believe this is the simplest solution with widespread browser support.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>The wrapper (<code>&lt;div&gt;</code> or <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> respectively) utilizes <code>min-width: 0</code> in order to shrink as needed.</p>
</li>
<li><p>The text functioning as a label uses <code>display: block;</code> to achieve the &quot;label over input&quot; look, and has <code>max-width: fit-content;</code> applied to prevent any excess space from being click-able.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>It was not mentioned in the question, but generally &quot;labels over inputs&quot; tend to be more applicable to a wider variety of forms, unlike &quot;labels beside inputs&quot; which can look odd when labels of different lengths are stacked up.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Removing labels entirely or using <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Attributes/placeholder#accessibility_concerns" rel="nofollow noreferrer">placeholders as labels</a> are not recommended due to accessibility concerns (doing so can lead to confusion for users with screen readers).</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>The input has <code>max-width: 100%</code>, which is currently better supported than <code>max-width: stretch</code>. By setting a specific width value via <code>width:</code>, the input will grow to fit that value or take up 100% of the viewport's width, whichever is smaller.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<h2>Notes</h2>
<ul>
<li><p>Copying and pasting the above snippet as-is will run into issues with labels overflowing because a method to handle them was not specified.</p>
<ul>
<li><p>I typically apply <code>overflow-wrap: anywhere</code> to the <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> element as a fallback to prevent overflow. The same value can be attached to the label/wrapper, but it may be more convenient to set this value once at the root of the webpage.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Alternatively, you can use <code>overflow: hidden</code> with <code>text-overflow: ellipsis</code> on the text labels to make them collapse with ellipses. However, I do not encourage this since it's possible that the labels may end up truncated to a point where their meanings cannot be easily deciphered. Overflowing labels might look ugly, but at least the information is still there for the user.</p>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li><p>When wrapping these inputs in a <code>&lt;fieldset&gt;</code>, the fieldset itself may overflow. I believe this is an issue specific to fieldsets. This can be addressed by applying <code>min-width: 0;</code> to it, allowing it to shrink like the input wrappers.</p>
</li>
<li><p>For a TailwindCSS version of the above label+input component, see the following:</p>
</li>
</ul>
<pre class="lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;label class=&quot;min-w-0&quot;&gt;
    &lt;span class=&quot;block max-w-fit&quot;&gt;Username&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;input type=&quot;text&quot; class=&quot;box-border max-w-full ...&quot;/&gt;
&lt;/label&gt;
</code></pre>
